<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Townsend Farquhar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a>–<a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Robert Townsend Farquhar was the first British governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1810. He governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a> until 1823, allowing the Franco-Mauritian planters to keep their law, language and religion. He signed treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, though slaves continued to be smuggled into the island.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Townsend Farquhar was born on 14 October 1776, the second son of the Scottish physician Walter Farquhar and his wife Anne, and was baptised at St James Piccadilly. He entered Westminster School in 1787, studied book-keeping under James Pierson and then joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, serving from 1795 as a writer at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>. By the late 1790s he was commercial resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Amboyna</a>, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colony seized in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>, where he made treaties of alliance and commerce with the sultans of Tidore, Ternate and Batchian that the Madras government later dissolved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>An attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlement of Ternate, launched beyond his brief, cost him the favour of the Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> and brought his demotion, but the Governor-General, Lord Wellesley, impressed by his letter of vindication, offered him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a>. As Lieutenant-Governor of Prince of Wales Island from 1 January 1804 he improved roads, water supply and fortifications and rebuilt Fort Cornwallis at a cost of $80,000, though many of his schemes were said to be near-impossible.</p><p>In 1807 he published a scheme to replace African slaves in the West India colonies with paid Chinese labourers, which won little support. In July 1810 he sailed with Commodore Josias Rowley's fleet against Île Bonaparte, now Réunion, and stayed as its governor, moving to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> in December after its capture under Admiral Albemarle Bertie. As governor of Mauritius from 4 December 1810 to 20 May 1823, with leave between 1817 and 1820, he had charts made of the region, encouraged trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, and sent naval forces against the French ships carrying slaves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Made a baronet on 27 July 1821, he sat in Parliament for Newton from 11 February 1825 and for Hythe from 1826, and from 1826 served by rotation as a director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. He died at his home in Richmond Terrace, Whitehall, on 16 March 1830. Farquhar Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-town-penang">George Town, Penang</a>, and the Farquhar Islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a> are named after him, and Patrick O'Brian portrayed him in The Mauritius Command as a capable political man.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Townsend Farquhar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The island was ceded in the 1814 Treaty of Paris, renamed Mauritius, and its first governor, <b>Robert Farquhar</b>, ruled from Port Louis.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-mauritius">British Mauritius</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1811" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Robert Townsend Farquhar"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1811">What was here in 1811? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
